# Go Concurrency

## Context

- Pass `context.Context` as first arg; derive with timeout/cancel for bounded operations.
- Check `ctx.Err()` in long loops and before expensive I/O.
- DBO: `fiber.Ctx` implements `context.Context` — pass it to services.

## Goroutines

- Every goroutine needs an owner and a stop signal (context cancel or `done` channel).
- No bare `go fn()` in library code without lifecycle management.
- Prefer `golang.org/x/sync/errgroup` for fan-out with error propagation.

```go
// ❌ BAD — orphaned goroutine
go processItems(items)

// ✅ GOOD
g, ctx := errgroup.WithContext(ctx)
for _, item := range items {
    g.Go(func() error {
        return processItem(ctx, item)
    })
}
return g.Wait()
```

## Channels

- Declare direction: `chan<- T` (send), `<-chan T` (receive).
- Close channels from the sender side only.
- Prefer context cancellation over channel close for shutdown signals.

## Shared State

- Protect shared mutable state with `sync.Mutex` or channels, never unsynchronized access.
- DBO `ConnectionManager` manages connection pool lifecycle with TTL cleanup — follow its pattern for new pools.

## init()

- Use `init()` only for registration (e.g. driver imports), never for business logic or DB calls.
